#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180473} :activity/id #uuid "0efc2539-64cd-569e-92f0-28478cfe49f4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049348 :topic/id #uuid "edd9f2d1-ecae-5cc2-8bee-e8544be84391" :topic/math-academy-id 3696} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180384 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179667 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179522 :question/id #uuid "b1f5f31f-55d0-5523-8225-03db04d35cb1" :question/math-academy-id "e-6248"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db8e7d68-5731-5a44-b86e-919a13689540" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050800}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180274} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284671} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345077}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Upper Riemann Sum for a Strictly Increasing Function"} :step/id #uuid "b3cd740c-76b6-54c2-b336-80c8655e0e32" :step/math-academy-id 23453 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180385}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180385 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179668 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179523 :question/id #uuid "7215d75e-c001-5c28-a5e7-08a74f6543d7" :question/math-academy-id "e-6250"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5c07033-ef84-52e6-bd08-b1f0cc289e9f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284672} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393528}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Upper Riemann Sum for a Strictly Decreasing Function"} :step/id #uuid "7f101beb-cb03-5c90-a80d-a4c60cfcea4a" :step/math-academy-id 23455 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180474}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180473 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180711 :tutorial/content "We know how to write the upper Riemann sum for a function $f(x,y)$ over a *fixed* rectangular partition. In this lesson, we will learn how to write down an expression for the upper Riemann sum for an *arbitrary* rectangular partition. \n\nAs a concrete example, let $R = [0,1] \\times [0,1]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and define the function $f(x,y)$ as \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x^2+y^2.\n$$\n\nFurther, let $P$ be an arbitrary partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}},\\,{P}_{2}={{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur goal is to find an expression for $U(f, P),$ the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition. We proceed identically to the case where $P$ was fixed.\n\nFirst, we consider an arbitrary subregion $R_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between $x_{i-1}$ and $x_i$ over the $x$-axis and $y_{j-1}$ and $y_j$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below. \n\n![](images/88/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nSince we're computing the *upper* sum, we're interested in the *maximum* value of $f$ in this subregion.\n\nNotice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = x^2+y^2$ increases as $x$ increases on $[0,1],$ and\n\n- $f(x,y) = x^2+y^2$ increases as $y$ increases on $[0,1].$\n\n![](images/67/67daa328cc6ae4cb37be386c327fb4548844fad2adc10476bdf1147c5a5d4218.png)\n\nTherefore, the *maximum* value of $f$ in the subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value of $f$ at the *top right corner* of the subregion.\n\n![](images/6e/6e58954fccd74eaf67c95df818c4c9050c35b324c5fffe4902a366cb295b8845.png)\n\nSo, if $M_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ in each subregion, we have\n\n$$\nM_{ij} = f(x_{i}, y_{j}) = x_{i}^2 + y_{j}^2\n$$\n\nfor all $1\\leq i\\leq m$ and $1\\leq j\\leq n.$ \n\nSince the area of $R_{ij}$ is $\\Delta x_i\\Delta y_j,$ the volume of the rectangular solid with base $R_{ij}$ and height $M_{ij}$ is\n\n$$\nM_{ij}\\Delta x_i\\Delta y_j = (x_{i}^2 + y_{j}^2)\\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j\n$$\n\nFinally, summing up all of our rectangular solids over the entire partition $P$, we find that the upper Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P) = \\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n (x_{i}^2 + y_{j}^2)\\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j \\, .\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4a36ae3f-6805-532b-857f-be7df7dceee3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5800 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b0cc1df4-f684-5378-a369-66eaed4c5117" :step/math-academy-id 35551 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180384}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180474 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179727 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179589 :question/id #uuid "79b4e8e3-5cfd-5b28-ba19-f69d2c9e0c71" :question/math-academy-id "e-9495"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b815d42d-8e8a-5036-b6c4-76e4c20b20b7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364385}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Upper Riemann Sum for a Function That’s Neither Strictly Increasing nor Decreasing"} :step/id #uuid "66e9e9d9-7c13-5958-ba9c-cefcdf240004" :step/math-academy-id 35552}] :activity/title "Upper Riemann Sums Over General Rectangular Partitions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179850}]}
